    "body": "## Summary\n\n**ADR-059 — Selector index for cascade performance.** One commit. **Docs follow after merge.**\n\nTargets the 1058ms cascade phase that ADR-058's split-timer measured. Delivers **5.3–5.6× speedup** on cascade — 189-200ms across three fresh Wikipedia loads.\n\n## The measurement (Release build, 3 fresh Wikipedia loads)\n\n| Run | Cascade | Speedup vs 1058ms |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 200.3ms | 5.3× |\n| 2 | 188.7ms | 5.6× |\n| 3 | 189.6ms | 5.6× |\n\n**Tight cluster, structural change, not noise.** Meets ADR-059's `PERF-CASCADE < 300ms` acceptance criterion.\n\n## What ships\n\n### `selectors.hpp/cpp`\n\n`AnchorBucket` enum (`Id`, `Class`, `Type`, `Universal`) + `anchor_for(selector)` returns the most selective mandatory-match simple from the rightmost compound.\n\n**Correct by construction** — if the anchor doesn't match the element, the rule can't match. This is the CSS specificity invariant that underpins every browser's selector index.\n\n### `stylesheet.hpp/cpp`\n\n`RuleIndex` struct with 4 buckets. **Stores `std::size_t` rule indices, not `Rule*` pointers** — critical because `apply_rule` uses iteration order as `source_order` tiebreaker; pointer-sort would break specificity ties. Natural numeric sort preserves original rule order.\n\n`build_index()` called at end of `parse()`. `candidates_for(id, classes, tag)` returns sorted + deduped candidates. **DOM-free interface** — no `dom::Element` include needed, callers extract id/classes/tag themselves.\n\n### `cascade.cpp`\n\nReplaced 6 rule-iteration loops with 2 candidate groups (UA + author). Candidates computed **once per element**, reused across 3 pseudo-target passes.\n\n```cpp\n// Before: for each element × 3 pseudo targets:\n//   for each rule in ua_sheet.rules: apply_rule\n//   for each rule in author_sheet.rules: apply_rule\n\n// After: for each element:\n//   ua_cands = ua_sheet.index.candidates_for(el_id, el_classes, el_tag);\n//   author_cands = author_sheet.index.candidates_for(...);\n//   for each pseudo target:\n//     for idx in ua_cands: apply_rule(ua_sheet.rules[idx], ...);\n//     for idx in author_cands: apply_rule(author_sheet.rules[idx], ...);\n```\n\n### `tests`\n\n- 3 `apply_rule_call_count` test sites: **assertions unchanged** — they use `< 100` bounds that cover both pre-ADR-059 (~17) and post-ADR-059 (~5) baselines. Only the comment updated to note the drop.\n- 5 new ADR-059 tests: bucket landings for #id/.class/tag/universal, plus multi-selector dedupe.\n\n## The 3 implementation lessons caught before shipping\n\n1. **Namespace-scope bug**: first `build_index` placement was appended after the closing `} // namespace` — compiled cleanly as a free function but `Stylesheet` was unreachable. Fix: relocated the block inside the namespace. Caught by the compiler immediately (22 errors, all the same root cause).\n\n2. **Test literal `\\n`**: Python's `'\\\\n'` in a heredoc appended literal `\\` + `n` characters to `test_stylesheet.cpp` instead of a newline. One-line fix.\n\n3. **Anchor priority is a real invariant**: `Id > Class > Type > Universal` — bucket by the most selective mandatory-match. Rules with multiple classes (`.a.b`) land in `by_class[a]`; element with class `b` only won't find them — but `.a` is required, so that's correct. **Missing anchors silently drop rules** — the 125 existing `test_stylesheet.cpp` tests caught nothing because the priority was right from the start.\n\n## The measured delta\n\n- **Cascade: 1058ms → ~190ms** (5.6×, saving ~870ms per page load)\n- **Layout: unchanged** (same code path as pre-ADR-059) — still 2.4s + environmental variance\n- **Total: cascade drops from 31% to 6-7% of render time**; layout is now 93% of remaining time\n\n**Honest note on layout variance**: layout measurements showed 3× variance across runs (2415–7156ms) for identical content in identical binary. This is environmental noise from concurrent Firefox (~900MB resident) + memory pressure on the 8GB test machine, not a regression. ADR-060 (layout perf) will need a careful multi-run methodology.\n\n## Deferred (documented in ADR-059 spec)\n\n- Pseudo-target partitioning (3× memory, not needed at this scale)\n- `[attribute]` indexing — attribute-selector rules fall into universal bucket; if Wikipedia's universal bucket is >500 rules, this could be a follow-up ADR-059b\n- Descendant combinator ancestor pre-filtering\n- **Layout's 2.4s** — ADR-060 territory\n\n## Test count\n\n- 675 → 680 cases (+5)\n- 2586 → 2608 assertions (+22)\n- Zero regression on 675 pre-existing tests\n\nRefs #258\n",
